Ovarian torsion
Acute Urgent Care
Sudden unilateral pelvic pain, Nausea/vomiting, Tender adnexal mass, Enlarged ovary without Doppler flow

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-042
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing, sharp pain in the lower abdomen accompanied by severe nausea and vomiting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young female presenting with sudden, severe, unilateral pelvic pain that escalated rapidly following a gym workout. The pain was accompanied by recurrent vomiting but no fever. This presentation strongly suggests an ischemic event such as ovarian torsion. Urgent, face-to-face assessment was critical to identify the need for an immediate transvaginal ultrasound and emergency surgical intervention to untwist the ovary and salvage its viability, as prolonged delay leads to irreversible necrosis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was doing squats, and suddenly this sharp, stabbing pain hit my left side so hard it dropped me to the floor, and now I can't stop throwing up.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden onset of severe, unilateral left lower abdominal and pelvic pain that began 2 hours ago during physical exercise. The pain was initially intermittent and colicky but has rapidly become severe and constant. The patient reports severe nausea and has experienced recurrent vomiting since the pain onset. The patient notes a sudden exacerbation of the pain when attempting to change positions or sit up. There is a complete absence of significant fever or preceding systemic inflammatory signs.
Functional Status: Lying on the couch in the fetal position, groaning in severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of irreversible loss of the affected ovary.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient is in obvious severe distress, pale from nausea, and clutching the left lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized guarding and rigidity in the left iliac fossa and suprapubic region, with marked tenderness on deep palpation. (Bimanual pelvic examination, if performed, would reveal a highly tender palpable left adnexal mass). Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia, secondary to the severe pain. There is no costovertebral angle tenderness. Expected investigations in the emergency department include a transvaginal ultrasound demonstrating an asymmetrically enlarged ovary greater than 4 cm with absent or decreased arterial and venous Doppler flow.
Environmental Observations: Vomit basin next to the couch.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took Paracetamol with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ovarian torsion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to a negative bedside urine pregnancy test and a regular menstrual history without abnormal vaginal bleeding.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain localizing strictly to the left side, the sudden (rather than gradual) onset, and the absence of fever or preceding periumbilical ache.

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain localizing entirely to the anterior pelvis without radiation to the flank or groin, and the absence of haematuria.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic diagnostic tetrad for ovarian torsion: sudden onset of severe unilateral pelvic pain, recurrent nausea/vomiting, suspected tender adnexal mass, and a history linked to positional change (exercise). The negative pregnancy test effectively rules out an ectopic event. Ovarian torsion is a time-critical surgical emergency. An urgent after-hours assessment allowed for the provision of strong parenteral analgesia and the immediate coordination of an ambulance transfer. Failing to urgently evaluate and refer this patient would guarantee complete ischemic necrosis of the ovary before the next morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate bedside urine pregnancy test (Result: Negative). Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg for severe pain and Ondansetron 4mg ODT for severe nausea. Kept patient strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM) in preparation for probable surgery. Called 000 for Priority 2 ambulance transport to a hospital with gynaecological surgery capabilities.
Positive Behavioral Change: By trusting your body and seeking emergency medical help the exact moment you felt this sudden, agonizing pain, you have given the surgical team the best possible chance to save your ovary.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and ED gynaecology team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The roommate is instructed to keep the patient resting in the fetal position. If the pain suddenly disappears entirely, followed by a high fever and generalized abdominal swelling, this may indicate the ovary has necrotized and ruptured.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your symptoms strongly point to your ovary twisting on its blood supply, which cuts off the oxygen. This requires an urgent ultrasound and likely surgery.
Clinical Course: The hospital will perform a specialized scan. If the ovary is twisted, they will take you to the operating room to untwist it via keyhole surgery to restore blood flow and save the tissue.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk around or change positions rapidly. Keep your stomach completely empty, as you will likely need general anesthesia within the next few hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong anti-inflammatory injection and an anti-nausea wafer to make the car ride bearable, but the physical twisting inside your pelvis can only be fixed with an operation.
Actionable Steps: Try to take slow, deep breaths to manage the pain. Your roommate will pack your identification and any phone chargers for the hospital stay.

